WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:05.359
Beautiful animation, catchy songs, and an
incredible performance from Robin Williams

00:00:05.359 --> 00:00:10.840
have made Aladdin a beloved kids' movie since
it hit theaters in 1992, but watching it as

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:12.720
a grownup can be a whole new world.

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:17.220
Here are the wild things going on behind the
fairy tale fun of Aladdin that you'll only

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:18.280
notice as an adult.

00:00:19.630 --> 00:00:23.880
Disney's animated offerings have a long history
of keeping their violence implied so that

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:26.180
the kids don't notice, but not Aladdin!

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:30.540
Not only is there a truly incredible amount
of gruesome stuff happening right there on

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:33.320
the screen, it's our hero who's responsible
for most of it.

00:00:34.330 --> 00:00:38.600
While Aladdin is cheerfully singing "One Jump
Ahead" — a song about how he has to steal

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:42.530
to avoid starving to death on the streets,
which is itself a little more grim than the

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:47.000
average Disney fare — the chase through
the bazaar has a level of carnage that most

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:48.780
hard-R movies would find hard to top.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:53.680
There's the poor guy who gets impaled on a
bed of nails after he's squashed by a guard,

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:57.980
a sword-swallower who has the blade yanked
out of his throat by Abu, and plenty of guards

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:01.700
who plunge several stories into a wooden cart
full of manure.

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:06.600
That might not be fatal, but it's almost certainly
unpleasant, and most likely results in plenty

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:07.200
of disease.

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:12.080
Considering all this happens in the first
ten minutes of the film, watching Aladdin

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:16.310
as an adult makes you realize pretty quickly
that this might not exactly be the children's

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:17.820
movie it's advertised as.

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:23.140
While we're on the subject of "One Jump Ahead,"
it seems pretty weird that Agrabah's police

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:27.970
force is willing to send dozens of armed men
after one guy (and one monkey) for the crime

00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:30.160
of of stealing a loaf of bread.

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:30.800
“Thief!

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:34.460
I'll have your hands for a trophy, street
rat!"

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:37.200
"All of this for a loaf of bread?"

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:42.190
As a kid, this seems like a fun romp to start
off the movie and establish Aladdin as a rogue

00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:46.490
with a heaert of gold, but as an adult, this
seems really suspicious.

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:48.270
Don't they have better things to do?

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:52.260
Like, for example, investigating the impending
coup being plotted by Jafar?

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:56.950
Or maybe they could devote some time to finding
the missing princess after she sneaks out

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:58.800
of the castle to mingle with the common people.

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:03.460
She leaves when it's dark and she's still
gone at sunset the next day, but nobody ever

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:04.860
goes to look for her.

00:02:05.230 --> 00:02:09.980
When she finally is found, it's just because
Jafar has sent the guards out to find Aladdin.

00:02:09.980 --> 00:02:14.160
Get your priorities in order, guys, and stop
leaving everything to the tiger.

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:19.760
When you're a kid, Abu seems like a cute,
lovable animal sidekick, but adults can pretty

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:21.920
quickly come to realize that Abu is actually…

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:24.380
“The woo-ooo-orst!"

00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:29.420
Abu is the one who is reluctant to give his
bread to starving children, even though they're

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:30.040
cute as all heck.

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:35.060
He's the one who steals a bunch of apples
and jewels even after Jasmine has already

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:37.360
been held at sword-point by a shopkeeper.

00:02:37.850 --> 00:02:41.620
He's the one who touches the treasure and
triggers the lava that forces Aladdin to flee

00:02:41.630 --> 00:02:47.000
for his life in the Cave of Wonders, and then,
on top of all that, he decides to cover Aladdin's

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:48.500
eyes as they try to fly away.

00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:54.080
Any adult watching the movie can tell you
that Abu is the worst animal sidekick in Aladdin,

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:58.760
and this is a movie where a talking parrot
is part of a conspiracy to commit regicide.

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:04.500
Don't worry: we're not going to go deep into
nitpicking the existence of the Magic Carpet.

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:08.440
It's magic, it flies, and accepting that is
just part of watching the movie, no matter

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:09.300
how old you are.

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:13.240
That said, while zipping through the clouds
on a trip around the world is fun enough for

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:18.020
the kids, adults who have a better understanding
of the distances involved are going to have

00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:21.160
a lot of questions about how fast that thing
can go.

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:26.320
In "A Whole New World," the Magic Carpet takes
Aladdin and Jasmine all around the globe in

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:29.500
a single one night, but doesn't seem like
it's going fast enough to even ruffle their

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:30.040
hair.

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:33.900
If we assume that Agrabah is somewhere in
the Middle East, let's say around Baghdad,

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:38.860
then a trip that goes to Giza for the Sphinx,
Athens for the Parthenon, the Forbidden City

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:44.260
in Beijing, and then back to the Middle East
covers a little bit over 10,000 miles.

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:48.690
Giving them the benefit of the doubt and assuming
that the whole trip covers a single eight-hour

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:53.550
night, we're talking about a throw rug that
can hit speeds of thirteen hundred miles per

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:57.740
hour, and that's if they didn't do anything
but fly at full power the whole time.

00:03:58.670 --> 00:04:02.580
Since Jasmine's hand isn't vaporized when
she dips it into the pond, the Carpet was

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:06.800
definitely going a little slower then, so
its maximum speed is even higher.

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:12.060
Forget about flying, the real magic here is
that Aladdin and Jasmine arrive back home

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:14.700
safe and sound without being turned to dust
by the friction.

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:17.540
Who knew the Magic Carpet was that powerful?

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:22.080
Everyone can agree that it would definitely
be a bad thing if Jafar took over Agrabah,

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:22.620
right?

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:26.620
From what we can see of his rule, in which
he makes some unsavory advances towards Jasmine

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:31.160
while the city becomes a fiery hellscape,
it would be a bad time for everyone.

00:04:31.820 --> 00:04:35.930
The question we have as adults, however, is
whether things are really that much better

00:04:35.930 --> 00:04:36.540
under the Sultan.

00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:43.140
"From now on, you’re to discuss sentencing
of prisoners with me, BEFORE they are beheaded."

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:46.060
“I assure you, your highness, it won't happen
again."

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:50.900
Jasmine's dad definitely seems like a really
nice person and he's a surprisingly compelling

00:04:51.260 --> 00:04:55.960
character, but he's been under Jafar's thumb
for so long that the bad guy is basically

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:56.960
running the place already.

00:04:57.460 --> 00:05:02.650
The Sultan is indecisive, anxious, childlike,
and fidgety, and it seems pretty unlikely

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:07.280
that he'd be able to inspire confidence in
his own people or negotiate with other rulers.

00:05:08.150 --> 00:05:12.820
Without someone like Jafar around to actually
run things — even if he's running them evilly

00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:16.920
— those next few years before Jasmine takes
the throne are going to be pretty rough.

